<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gunpowder Plot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1605">1605</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Gunpowder Treason Plot, Jesuit Treason</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">Gunpowder</a> Plot of 1605 was an unsuccessful attempt by a group of English Catholics, led by Robert Catesby, to kill King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> and I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The plan was to blow up the House of Lords on 5 November 1605, as the prelude to a revolt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> in which the king's nine-year-old daughter, Princess Elizabeth, was to be installed as head of state. After an anonymous letter of warning reached William Parker, 4th Baron Monteagle, a search on 4 November found Guy Fawkes guarding 36 barrels of gunpowder. Catesby was shot dead at Holbeche House; eight surviving conspirators, including Fawkes, were convicted in January 1606 and sentenced to be hanged, drawn and quartered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gunpowder-plot.jpg" alt="Gunpowder Plot" width="330" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (printed for P. Brooksby, I. Deacon, I. Blare, I. Back.) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GunpowderPlot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
